    "prompt": "\u3010Generation Goal\u3011\nGenerate a photoreal continuation of the cabinet assembly in which the side walls close by a very small amount to lock the box squarely.\n\n\u3010Reference Asset Roles\u3011\n@Video1 is a coarse-blockout reference. Use it only for the precise small inward motion of the two long side walls, fixed base, component blocking, action timing, and static camera; do not adopt its proxy geometry, simplified materials, empty scene, or lack of people.\n@Image1 defines the white cabinet materials, orange CLICKREADY connectors, dark work mat, light tile floor, and bright laundry-room appearance; do not change the motion defined by @Video1.\n\n\u3010Subjects and Relationships\u3011\nThe woman kneels at the near edge with her left hand on the left long side wall and her right hand on the right long side wall. The man kneels opposite and keeps the cabinet stable. The base, front frame, installed end wall, and loose pieces remain stationary.\nThe two people are the SAME throughout: a woman in her early 30s with shoulder-length dark-brown hair in a plain cream knit sweater and blue jeans, and a man in his early 50s with short salt-and-pepper grey hair in a plain light-blue polo and blue jeans. Bright modern laundry room, grey wainscoting, light tile floor, open wood shelves, dark front-load washer/dryer. Plain clothing, no text or logos.\n\n\u3010Event Script\u3011\nAt the start, the cabinet lies flat on the mat in the same wide rectangular shape shown in @Video1: its side-to-side width remains visibly greater than its front-to-back depth. The base, front frame, shorter installed end wall, and loose pieces remain stationary. Following @Video1, the woman keeps her left hand on the left long wall and her right hand on the right long wall and presses only those two walls inward by a very small amount. The man keeps both hands on the shorter installed end wall, holding it stationary. Every panel remains rigid, and no component moves unless a visible hand is supporting and moving it. At the end, the securely connected cabinet remains visibly rectangular and all components are stationary.\n\n\u3010Maintain Consistency\u3011\nKeep the same two people, cabinet component count, white panels, orange connectors, room layout, camera axis, and physical hand-to-panel contact. Preserve rigid panel dimensions; do not bend, duplicate, collapse, or broadly swing the walls.\n",
